{"content":"Unfortunately, I'm going to reject this patch in it's state (as well as the child patch) for the following reasons:


There are a lot of missing assertion messages in the tests
The JSDoc for the functions are either vague or just read like a function acceptance requirement. Instead they should provide a general description with code comments within the function describing when those "requirements" are happening. Examples:
Read usable calendars in calendar-list order and the user's default. (default what? what is calendar-list order? What are we reading, doesn't the function just "get" the calendars? )
Apply an edit or move and send its notifications. If source deletion fails during a move, remove the unchanged destination copy and rethrow the source error. Preserve copies changed by other edits. Log any cleanup failure and retain the copy for cleanup on retry.  Before another edit, remove unchanged copies left by failed moves for the same source item. Preserve copies changed by other edits. (These sound like function requirements)

The variable, type, and function naming is vague and sometimes confusing (eg. CalendarEventCalendar?)
This patch has the #renderCalendar function doing way too much.
The code (especially the test code) does not have any logical spacing in it making it very difficult to read, as well as 0 code comments (this is something that helps the team greatly when trying to parse the code)
Layers of conditionals , which is difficult to read and understand. Eg.


if (
      options.length != this.#calendarSelect.options.length ||
      options.some((option, index) => {
        const current = this.#calendarSelect.options[index];
        return (
          option.value != current.value ||
          option.textContent != current.textContent ||
          option.disabled != current.disabled
        );
      })
    )

I would recommend marking the child patch as a WIP and going through each patch and ensuring they are up to your own standards for submitting code and consider the teams needs when they have to hop in to those files to read the code, leading to less back and forth with revisions. I don't feel like I'm being to picky here, but maybe @tobyp or @klamping or someone can take a look, otherwise happy to chat with some of the team to get on the same page with this stuff.","codeSuggestion":"","assessment":"The reviewer asks for assertion messages, clearer documentation and names, smaller rendering steps, logical spacing, useful comments, and further child-patch work. The selected patch now addresses the assertion messages, default-calendar documentation, formatting, and spacing. Discuss the remaining naming and helper requests. No further source change is established for D332509. The child assertion omissions remain open in their owning patches.","rationale":"loadCalendarOptions now names the ordering preference, supplied default ID, nullable result, and separate unfiltered default snapshot. CalendarEventCalendar explicitly describes display data with id, name, and color. #renderCalendar ties each control to its operation and preserves focus. Its option comparison avoids replacing unchanged options. The unit test confirms rejection of old selections and stable selector results for unrelated field changes. The quoted move-cleanup documentation belongs to the child transaction code, where the current comments explain revision checks and retry behavior.","recommendation":"discussion","validation":"The selected unit test passed all 23 assertions. Source checks found messages for all assertions in both new selected-patch test files. Inspected test_focusAfterOperationReplacement, test_focusDuringOperationLoading, test_oldDefaultAndSelectionCannotChangeNewOperation, and the descendant Save tests. Descendant files were read with git show and were not executed. Both headless browser attempts stopped before tests, so focus and accessibility behavior remain unverified.","appliedSummary":"","changeSummary":"","state":"handled","changeAccepted":false,"changeReverted":false,"changesAmended":false}
